steps.body:
Every request that reaches Roombook either finds the one handler meant for it, or gets a clear answer that it took a wrong turn. That's routing, and getting it right is what makes an API predictable: the same path always means the same thing, and a mistake gets the status code that says exactly what was wrong.

## Method plus pattern

A **route** pairs a method with a **path pattern** and points to a handler. A pattern can have a **path parameter**, a named piece that stands for any value: in Express, `/v1/rooms/:roomId` matches `/v1/rooms/3` and `/v1/rooms/42`, and hands your handler the 3 or the 42 as `roomId`. The app's whole list of routes is its **route table**, and Express checks a request against it to pick a handler.

```diagram routes-and-methods-route-table
The request matches one row by its method and its path, and that row's handler runs.
```

The diagram writes the parameter in braces, `{roomId}`, the way API documentation does, and Express writes the same pattern as `:roomId`.

## Routers and /v1

Roombook keeps each feature's routes in its own file, in an Express **router**: a small group of routes that you attach to the app at a path of your choosing. Here's `src/modules/rooms/rooms.routes.ts`, shortened to its shape:

```ts sketch
export const roomRoutes = Router();

roomRoutes
  .route("/")
  .get(/* list rooms */)
  .post(/* create a room */)
  .all(methodNotAllowed("GET, HEAD, POST"));

roomRoutes
  .route("/:roomId")
  .get(/* read one room */)
  .put(/* replace it */)
  .patch(/* change some fields */)
  .delete(/* delete it */)
  .all(methodNotAllowed("GET, HEAD, PUT, PATCH, DELETE"));
```

`Router()` makes an empty router, and `roomRoutes.route(path)` names a path once, so each method after it adds that method's handler. The router's paths are relative: `app.ts` attaches it under `/v1/rooms`, so `"/:roomId"` becomes `/v1/rooms/:roomId`:

```ts
app.use("/v1/rooms", roomRoutes);
app.use("/v1/bookings", bookingRoutes);
```

Every Roombook route sits under `/v1`, for version 1. When a change would break existing clients one day, a `/v2` can live next to it while they move over, which the lesson on curl, Postman, and OpenAPI covers. `/healthz` stays outside, since it isn't part of the API's contract.

## Reading a path parameter

Express puts path parameters in `req.params`, and they're always strings: for `/v1/rooms/3`, `req.params.roomId` is `"3"`. Roombook's ids are whole numbers, so `src/http/ids.ts` turns the text into one, or into `undefined` when it isn't a valid id:

```ts
export function parseId(text: string): number | undefined {
  const id = Number(text);
  return /^[1-9][0-9]*$/.test(text) && Number.isSafeInteger(id) ? id : undefined;
}
```

The regular expression, `/^[1-9][0-9]*$/`, accepts digits only, with no leading zero, so `abc`, `-1`, `1.5`, and `007` all fail. `Number.isSafeInteger` rejects numbers too big for JavaScript to hold exactly. An id that fails gets **404 Not Found**, the same as an id that's valid but missing. From the client's side, `/v1/rooms/abc` is simply an address where nothing lives.

Every 404 has the same small JSON body for now, `{"status": 404, "title": "Room not found"}`, sent with `res.status(404).json(...)`. It's a first draft of Roombook's error format. The lesson on validation and errors turns it into the full Problem Details shape you saw in What an API Is and What It's For.

## 404 or 405

A request can miss in two ways, and they deserve two answers. If no pattern matches the path, it's 404. If the path matches but not the method, like `POST /v1/rooms/1`, the thing exists and the method is wrong, and the answer is **405 Method Not Allowed**, with an `Allow` header listing the methods that do work.

Express doesn't send 405 by itself, which surprises many people: it keeps looking for another route, finds none, and answers 404. That's what the `.all(methodNotAllowed(...))` at the end of each route is for. `.all` matches every method, and since it comes last, it only runs when no method above it did. `src/http/method-not-allowed.ts`:

```ts
export function methodNotAllowed(allow: string): RequestHandler {
  return (req, res) => {
    res.set("Allow", allow);
    if (req.method === "OPTIONS") {
      res.status(204).end();
      return;
    }
    res.status(405).json({ status: 405, title: "Method Not Allowed" });
  };
}
```

It answers OPTIONS, a method that asks which methods a path supports, with 204 and the same `Allow` list, since that's not an error. The part on DELETE says more about OPTIONS. Any other method gets a 405, with the same kind of JSON body as a 404.

## Order matters

Express checks routes in the order you add them, and the first match wins. A parameter matches any text, so a fixed path added after it never runs. If Roombook added `/popular` after `/:roomId`, a request for `/v1/rooms/popular` would reach the room handler with `roomId` set to `"popular"`, and get a 404. Add fixed paths before patterns with parameters.

Express 5 also changed its path syntax from Express 4. A wildcard needs a name, like `/*splat`, an optional piece goes in braces, like `/rooms{/:roomId}`, and characters like `?` and `+` are no longer allowed in a path string.

Most of an API's traffic is reads: people look far more often than they change anything. And every program between your API and its clients, from browsers to caches, treats a GET as harmless. They repeat it, prefetch it, and keep copies of it. So the rules for reads are the ones you can least afford to break.

## Read one, read many

Roombook has two reads for rooms. `GET /v1/rooms/:roomId` answers **200 OK** with one room, or 404. `GET /v1/rooms` answers 200 with every room:

```ts
roomRoutes
  .route("/")
  .get((_req, res) => {
    res.json({ items: rooms.list() });
  });
```

The list is wrapped in an object, `{"items": [...]}`, instead of being sent as a bare array. That leaves room to add fields next to it later without breaking anyone: the next lesson adds `nextCursor`, for reading a long list a page at a time. A bare array can't grow that way. An empty list is still a success: 200 with `{"items": []}`, never 404, because the list exists, it just has nothing in it yet.

Here's the handler for one room:

```ts sketch
.get((req, res) => {
  const room = rooms.findById(parseId(req.params.roomId));
  if (!room) {
    res.status(404).json({ status: 404, title: "Room not found" });
    return;
  }
  res.json(room);
})
```

The `return` after the 404 matters. Without it, the handler would carry on to `res.json` and try to send a second response, and Node.js would throw `Cannot set headers after they are sent to the client`. Every path through a handler should send exactly one response.

## Where the rooms live, for now

`rooms.findById` and `rooms.list` come from `src/modules/rooms/rooms.repository.ts`. A **repository** is the one place in the code that stores and fetches a kind of thing, here rooms, with methods named for what they do: `list`, `findById`, `insert`, `update`, and `remove`. For now it keeps the rooms in memory, in a `Map` from id to room, filled with Roombook's three rooms when the server starts:

```ts
const rooms = new Map<number, Room>([
  [1, { id: 1, name: "Turing", building: "main-library", capacity: 6, amenities: ["whiteboard", "screen"] }],
  [2, { id: 2, name: "Lovelace", building: "main-library", capacity: 4, amenities: ["whiteboard"] }],
  [3, { id: 3, name: "Hopper", building: "science-library", capacity: 8, amenities: ["screen", "video-call"] }],
]);
```

Memory forgets everything when the server restarts, which `--watch` does on every save, so your changes vanish each time. That's fine while you learn routing. The lesson on services and repositories explains why repositories are a separate layer, and the raw SQL lesson swaps this one for PostgreSQL without touching the routes.

## Safe means no changes

Recall from the web course that GET is **safe**: it only reads. For an API, this is a promise other programs rely on. A chat app fetches every link someone pastes to show a preview. A browser may load links before you click them, to be quick. A client retries a GET that timed out. If `GET /v1/bookings/1001/cancel` cancelled a booking, pasting that link in a group chat would cancel it. So a GET never changes anything a client can see. Writing a log line, or counting visits, is fine.

## HEAD

**HEAD** asks for exactly what GET would answer, without the body: the same status and the same headers. A client uses it to check that something exists, or how big it is, without downloading it. Express answers HEAD with your GET handler and throws the body away, so you get it for free. That's why Roombook's `Allow` lists include HEAD next to GET.

## 200 or 404

A read answers 200 with the thing, or 404 when it isn't there. Later lessons add two more: 304 Not Modified, when the client's copy is still current, in the next lesson, and 403 or 404 for something the caller isn't allowed to see, in the lesson on permissions.

Creating is where a client most needs a clear answer: did it work, what's the new thing's id, and where can it be read later. POST is also the one common method that's never safe to repeat, which makes it the method behind most double bookings and double charges on the web.

## Create: 201 with Location

`POST /v1/rooms` creates a room from the JSON in the request's body:

```ts sketch
.post((req, res) => {
  const { name, building, capacity, amenities } = req.body;
  const room = rooms.insert({ name, building, capacity, amenities });
  res.status(201).location(`/v1/rooms/${room.id}`).json(room);
})
```

`req.body` is the request's JSON, already turned into an object. That's done by one line in `app.ts`, `app.use(express.json(...))`, which reads the body of every request that sends JSON. The next lesson covers bodies in full, and the middleware lesson explains what `app.use` does.

The handler picks out the four fields a new room has, by name, instead of passing `req.body` along whole. That way a client can't slip in `"id": 1` and overwrite room 1, or set any field the API never meant to accept. It doesn't check the values yet: a capacity of `"lots"` would be stored as it is. The lesson on validation and errors checks every field.

The answer has three parts, and clients use all of them:

- **201 Created**: something new exists.
- A `Location` header with the new room's address, `/v1/rooms/4`, where the client can read it from now on.
- The new room in the body, so the client learns its id, and any value the server filled in, without a second request.

## POST isn't idempotent

Recall from the web course that sending the same POST twice does the work twice. Two identical `POST /v1/rooms` requests make two rooms. For bookings, it's worse. A student taps Book on a train, the request reaches Roombook, the booking is made, and the answer is lost in a tunnel. The app sees a timeout and sends the request again, and now the student holds two bookings. The lesson on retries and idempotency fixes this with an `Idempotency-Key` header, so Roombook can tell a retry from a new request.

## Actions: POST /v1/bookings/:bookingId/cancel

Some changes aren't creating a thing or editing a field. Cancelling a booking has rules: only before it starts, only by its owner or staff in that building. It also has effects: the room frees up, and an email goes out. Roombook models it as an **action**: a POST to a path named with a verb, under the booking it acts on.

```ts
bookingRoutes
  .route("/:bookingId/cancel")
  .post((req, res) => {
    const booking = bookings.findById(parseId(req.params.bookingId));
    if (!booking) {
      res.status(404).json({ status: 404, title: "Booking not found" });
      return;
    }
    if (booking.status === "cancelled") {
      res.json(booking);
      return;
    }
    res.json(bookings.cancel(booking));
  })
  .all(methodNotAllowed("POST"));
```

Why not the other methods? Not DELETE, because the booking isn't gone: it stays, with its status set to `cancelled`, so the history shows it. Not `PATCH {"status": "cancelled"}`, because then a client could just as easily send `{"status": "confirmed"}` to bring back a booking whose slot someone else now holds. An action runs the process with its rules, and a PATCH edits fields. The answer is 200 with the cancelled booking.

`bookings.cancel` sets the status to `cancelled` and adds 1 to the booking's `version`, as every change does, which the lesson on constraints and transactions uses to catch two edits that clash. This action is safe to retry, even though POST in general isn't: a booking that's already cancelled comes back as it is, unchanged. Design actions this way whenever you can.

## 202: finished later

Some work takes too long to finish while the client waits, like exporting a whole term of bookings to a spreadsheet. The API answers **202 Accepted** at once, with a `Location` pointing at a resource that reports progress. The client checks that address until the work is done, and it then links to the result. Roombook's booking emails work the same way inside the server, with no 202 to the client: the booking gets its 201 straight away, and the email goes out moments later, through the background jobs of the Redis lesson.

There are two ways to change something that exists, and they behave very differently. Use the wrong one, or build one wrong, and a client that meant to change one field wipes out three others. Knowing exactly what each one promises is what lets clients update safely.

## PUT replaces the whole thing

A **PUT** sends a complete new version of the resource, and the server stores exactly that, in place of the old one:

```ts sketch
.put((req, res) => {
  const room = rooms.findById(parseId(req.params.roomId));
  if (!room) {
    res.status(404).json({ status: 404, title: "Room not found" });
    return;
  }
  const { name, building, capacity, amenities } = req.body;
  res.json(rooms.update({ id: room.id, name, building, capacity, amenities }));
})
```

Every field comes from the body, and only the id comes from the room that's there, so the path decides which room, never the body. A field the client leaves out is gone, as the third example shows: a PUT with only a name leaves a room with no capacity at all. A real API refuses a PUT that's missing required fields, with 422, and the lesson on validation and errors adds that check.

PUT is **idempotent**: sending the same PUT twice leaves the room exactly as one did. That makes it safe for a client to retry.

## PATCH changes some fields

A **PATCH** sends only the changes. Roombook follows **JSON Merge Patch**, a standard set of rules for what a JSON patch means, written down as RFC 7396, one of the IETF's standards documents you met in What an API Is and What It's For. Its rules:

- A field in the patch replaces the current value.
- A field left out stays as it is.
- A field set to `null` is removed, or cleared.
- Arrays are replaced whole. To add an amenity, you send the full new list.
- Objects inside the resource are merged field by field, by the same rules.

A merge patch announces itself with `Content-Type: application/merge-patch+json`. That's why `app.ts` gives `express.json` a `type` list: by default it only reads `application/json`, and Roombook accepts both.

```ts sketch
.patch((req, res) => {
  const room = rooms.findById(parseId(req.params.roomId));
  if (!room) {
    res.status(404).json({ status: 404, title: "Room not found" });
    return;
  }
  const { name, building, capacity, amenities } = req.body;
  res.json(
    rooms.update({
      id: room.id,
      name: name ?? room.name,
      building: building ?? room.building,
      capacity: capacity ?? room.capacity,
      amenities: amenities ?? room.amenities,
    }),
  );
})
```

For each field, `??` keeps the current value when the patch doesn't have one.

## Missing or null

JSON can say two different things about a field: it's not there, or it's there and `null`. In a merge patch they mean different things: `{}` changes nothing, and `{"note": null}` clears a booking's note. JavaScript keeps them apart too, as `undefined` and `null`, but `??` treats both the same, which is fine for a room, since none of its fields may ever be null. The lesson on validation and errors turns `{"capacity": null}` into a clear error. For a field that can be cleared, like a booking's note, the handler has to ask whether the field is in the body at all, and the next lesson shows how.

## Is PATCH idempotent?

A merge patch is: sending `{"capacity": 8}` twice still leaves 8. But HTTP doesn't promise it for PATCH in general, since a patch format could say "add 2 to the capacity", which changes the result every time. So the method table in the next part says PATCH isn't idempotent, and clients don't retry one blindly.

## 200 or 204

After an update, an API answers either **200** with the updated resource, or **204 No Content** with no body. Roombook answers 200, so the client sees exactly what's stored, including anything the server changed, like the version number the lesson on constraints and transactions adds. Pick one and use it for every update.
